<article class="wp-block-group curio-single__article is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow"><header class="curio-article-header curio-article-header--default no-media wp-block-curio-article-header">
	<div class="curio-article-header__content">
					<nav class="curio-article-header__terms has-parent" aria-label="Section"><a class="curio-article-header__parent" href="https://biologyinsights.com/category/medicine/">Medicine</a><span class="curio-article-header__sep" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span><a class="curio-article-header__eyebrow" href="https://biologyinsights.com/category/medicine/safety/">Safety</a></nav>
				<h1 class="curio-article-header__title">Lip Trauma: First Aid, Treatment, and Healing Process</h1>
					<p class="curio-article-header__deck">Explore a complete guide to understanding and navigating lip injuries, from initial care considerations to supporting a healthy recovery.</p>
			</div>
	</header>


<div class="curio-article-meta curio-article-meta--with-sidebar wp-block-curio-article-byline">
				<div class="curio-article-byline has-cite">
							<div class="curio-article-byline__row">
											<p class="curio-article-byline__authors">By <a href="https://biologyinsights.com/author/gretchen-l-gaines/" title="Posts by Gretchen L. Gaines">Gretchen L. Gaines</a></p>
										<div class="curio-citebox" data-curio-citebox><button type="button" class="curio-citebox__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-citebox-2" data-curio-citebox-toggle><svg data-stroke="1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="curio-icon curio-icon--quote" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M16 3a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v6a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 1 1 0 0 1 1 1v1a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 1 1 0 0 0-1 1v2a1 1 0 0 0 1 1 6 6 0 0 0 6-6V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2z" /><path d="M5 3a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v6a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 1 1 0 0 1 1 1v1a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 1 1 0 0 0-1 1v2a1 1 0 0 0 1 1 6 6 0 0 0 6-6V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2z" /></svg><span>Cite</span></button><div class="curio-citebox__panel" id="curio-citebox-2" role="group" aria-labelledby="curio-citebox-2-h" hidden><div class="curio-citebox__top"><h2 class="curio-citebox__heading" id="curio-citebox-2-h">Cite this article</h2><button type="button" class="curio-citebox__close" data-curio-citebox-close><svg data-stroke="1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="curio-icon curio-icon--close" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M18 6 6 18" /><path d="m6 6 12 12" /></svg><span class="screen-reader-text">Close</span></button></div><label class="curio-citebox__label" for="curio-citebox-2-style">Citation style</label><span class="curio-citebox__select-wrap"><select class="curio-citebox__select" id="curio-citebox-2-style" data-curio-citebox-style><option value="apa">APA</option><option value="mla">MLA</option><option value="chicago">Chicago</option><option value="harvard">Harvard</option></select><svg data-stroke="1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="curio-icon curio-icon--chevron-down curio-citebox__select-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg></span><p class="curio-citebox__text" data-curio-citebox-text="apa">L. Gaines, G. (2026, October 2). <i>Lip Trauma: First Aid, Treatment, and Healing Process</i>. Biology Insights. https://biologyinsights.com/lip-trauma-first-aid-treatment-and-healing-process/</p><p class="curio-citebox__text" data-curio-citebox-text="mla" hidden>L. Gaines, Gretchen. “Lip Trauma: First Aid, Treatment, and Healing Process.” <i>Biology Insights</i>, 2 Oct. 2026, biologyinsights.com/lip-trauma-first-aid-treatment-and-healing-process/. Accessed <span data-curio-citebox-accessed="mla">22 Nov. 2026</span>.</p><p class="curio-citebox__text" data-curio-citebox-text="chicago" hidden>L. Gaines, Gretchen. “Lip Trauma: First Aid, Treatment, and Healing Process.” <i>Biology Insights</i>, October 2, 2026. https://biologyinsights.com/lip-trauma-first-aid-treatment-and-healing-process/.</p><p class="curio-citebox__text" data-curio-citebox-text="harvard" hidden>L. Gaines, G. (2026) <i>Lip Trauma: First Aid, Treatment, and Healing Process</i>. Biology Insights. Available at: https://biologyinsights.com/lip-trauma-first-aid-treatment-and-healing-process/ (Accessed: <span data-curio-citebox-accessed="harvard">22 November 2026</span>).</p><button type="button" class="curio-citebox__copy" data-curio-citebox-copy data-done="Copied"><svg data-stroke="1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="curio-icon curio-icon--copy" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="8" y="8" width="14" height="14" rx="2" ry="2" /><path d="M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2" /></svg><span>Copy citation</span></button><span class="screen-reader-text" role="status" data-curio-citebox-status data-message="Citation copied"></span><p class="curio-citebox__note">Check the format against the style guide you’re using.</p></div></div>				</div>
						<p class="curio-article-byline__date">
				<time class="curio-article-byline__time" datetime="2026-10-02T19:40:15-05:00">October 2, 2026</time>			</p>
		</div>
		</div>


<div class="curio-article-body curio-article-body--post wp-block-curio-article-body">
	<div class="curio-article-body__content">
					<div class="curio-rich-text curio-article-body__text entry-content post-287738 post type-post status-publish format-standard category-safety grow-content-main mv-content-wrapper"><p>Lip injuries rank among the most common facial traumas seen in emergency departments, caused by everything from playground falls to car accidents to dog bites. The good news is that lips heal remarkably well compared to skin elsewhere on the body, thanks to an exceptionally rich blood supply and the unique biology of oral tissue. That said, proper first aid in the first minutes matters, and certain types of lip wounds need professional repair to avoid lasting cosmetic problems. Here is what you need to know about caring for a lip injury from the moment it happens through full recovery.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio1 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="8340708808"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>What to Do in the First Few Minutes</h2>
<p>Lip injuries bleed a lot. The lips receive blood from a dense network of arteries running just a few millimeters below the surface, so even a small cut can produce dramatic bleeding that looks worse than it is. Your first priority is to control that bleeding by applying firm, steady pressure with a clean cloth or gauze. Hold it for at least ten minutes without peeking, since lifting the cloth too soon restarts the clotting process.</p>
<p>Once bleeding slows, gently clean the wound with clean running water. If the cut is on the outside of the lip, a mild soap around (not directly inside) the wound helps remove debris. For cuts inside the mouth, a gentle rinse with clean water works. Remove any visible dirt, gravel, or fragments, but do not probe deeply into the wound yourself. Apply a cold compress or wrapped ice pack to the area for ten to fifteen minutes to reduce swelling. Over-the-counter pain relief like ibuprofen or acetaminophen can help with both pain and inflammation.</p>
<p>A minor lip cut that is shallow, stops bleeding with pressure, and does not cross the border between the colored part of the lip and the surrounding skin will usually heal fine on its own with basic wound care. Keep it clean, avoid picking at any scab that forms, and apply a thin layer of petroleum jelly to keep the wound moist while it closes.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio2 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="8245340034"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>When You Need to See a Doctor</h2>
<p>Not every lip injury can be managed at home. Several features signal that a wound needs professional attention, sometimes urgently.</p>
<p>The most critical landmark on the lip is the vermilion border, the sharp line where the red-colored lip meets the surrounding skin. Lacerations that cross this border require precise alignment during closure. Even a one- to two-millimeter mismatch at the vermilion border is visible at conversational distance, so these wounds should be repaired by a clinician experienced in facial suturing. Lacerations involving more than about a quarter of the lip&#8217;s width are particularly challenging and prone to deformity if not carefully repaired.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="1"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-1" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-3" aria-label="Citation 1">1</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-3" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34397497/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lip Laceration and Vermilion Border Repair</a></span></p>
<p>Other reasons to seek care include wounds that go all the way through the lip (entering the mouth), any wound with visible muscle or fat, injuries caused by animal or human bites, lacerations with embedded debris you cannot remove, wounds that will not stop bleeding after twenty minutes of direct pressure, and any injury associated with loose or broken teeth. If the wound is on a young child who cannot keep the area clean or still, professional closure is usually the safer choice.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio3 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="3407375501"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>How Lip Lacerations Are Repaired</h2>
<p>When a lip laceration needs stitches, the approach a clinician takes depends on the wound&#8217;s depth, location, and whether it crosses key landmarks.</p>
<p>Before anything else, the wound needs effective numbing. Traditional local anesthesia, where numbing medication is injected directly into the wound edges, can swell the tissue and distort important landmarks like the vermilion border. Many emergency and plastic surgery providers now prefer nerve blocks instead. A mental nerve block numbs the lower lip, and an infraorbital nerve block numbs the upper lip, both without injecting into the wound itself.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="2"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-2" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-4" aria-label="Citation 2">2</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-4" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21086229/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lip and perioral trauma</a></span> A study comparing the two approaches found that nerve blocks required roughly half the anesthetic volume, produced significantly lower pain scores, and preserved the natural tissue contours far better than direct injection.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="3"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-3" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-5" aria-label="Citation 3">3</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-5" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39810002/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aesthetic Unit Preservation in Emergency Facial Lacerations: A Prospective Evaluation of Landmark-Based Nerve Blocks</a></span></p>
<p>The lips consist of three distinct layers: outer skin, the orbicularis oris muscle in the middle, and inner oral mucosa. A full-thickness laceration, one that goes all the way through, requires repair of each layer separately. The clinician typically starts by precisely aligning the vermilion border with the first suture, since that landmark is the most visible. Muscle is then repaired to restore function, followed by closure of the inner mucosal layer and the outer skin. Intraoral sutures are usually made with absorbable material that dissolves on its own, while outer stitches on the skin side are typically removed after four to five days to prevent visible suture marks from forming.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="4"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-4" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-6" aria-label="Citation 4">4</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-6" role="note" hidden>Emergency Medicine Clinics of North America. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0733-8627(05)70139-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lacerations of the Mouth &#8211; Section: Wound Dressing and Care</a></span></p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio4 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="9437597239"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>Why Lips Heal Better Than Most Skin</h2>
<p>If you have ever noticed that a cut inside your mouth seems to vanish in days while a scrape on your arm lingers for weeks, you are not imagining things. The oral mucosa, the moist tissue lining the inside of your mouth and the inner surface of your lips, heals faster and with less scarring than skin almost anywhere else on the body.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="5"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-5" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-7" aria-label="Citation 5">5</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-7" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8394648/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Bigger Picture: Why Oral Mucosa Heals Better Than Skin</a></span></p>
<p>Several factors drive this advantage. The oral environment stays consistently moist, which promotes cell migration across the wound surface. Saliva contains growth factors and antimicrobial compounds that actively support healing. The immune response in oral tissue resolves more quickly, meaning less prolonged inflammation and less of the excessive collagen buildup that creates thick scars. Research comparing matched wounds in skin and oral mucosa found significantly reduced scar formation in the mouth at both the visible and microscopic level, with skin wounds showing higher levels of collagen-producing cells and scar-associated proteins.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="6"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-6" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-8" aria-label="Citation 6">6</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-8" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19769724/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wound healing in oral mucosa results in reduced scar formation as compared with skin: evidence from the red Duroc pig model and humans</a></span> Oral mucosal wounds also showed faster resolution of inflammation and better control of the cells responsible for wound contraction, both key reasons why mouth wounds heal with minimal scarring.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="7"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-7" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-9" aria-label="Citation 7">7</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-9" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19854029/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scarless healing of oral mucosa is characterized by faster resolution of inflammation and control of myofibroblast action compared to skin wounds in the red Duroc pig model</a></span></p>
<p>The outer skin surface of the lip does not share all of these advantages, so cuts on the external lip side heal more like regular skin wounds. Still, the lip&#8217;s outstanding blood supply gives even outer lip wounds a healing edge over lacerations on less vascular body parts. Both the superior and inferior labial arteries run through the lip tissue at an average depth of roughly five millimeters, and the arteries course most commonly through the submucosal plane, keeping the tissue well-oxygenated during repair.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="8"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-8" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-10" aria-label="Citation 8">8</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-10" role="note" hidden>SpringerLink (Aesthetic Plastic Surgery). <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42329443/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Blood Supply of the Lips: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis</a></span></p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio5 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="6811433898"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>Animal Bites to the Lip</h2>
<p>Dog bites are a leading cause of lip lacerations, and the lips are the most commonly affected site in head-and-face bite injuries.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="9"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-9" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-11" aria-label="Citation 9">9</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-11" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16055237/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Animal bite injuries to the head: 132 cases</a></span> Bite wounds carry a higher infection risk than clean-cut lacerations because animal mouths harbor a mix of bacteria that gets driven deep into the tissue. Despite that risk, current practice favors immediate primary closure of facial bite wounds rather than leaving them open to heal on their own. Thorough wound cleaning and irrigation, combined with antibiotic prophylaxis, yields good cosmetic results with a low infection rate.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="10"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-10" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-12" aria-label="Citation 10">10</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-12" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5493545/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Animal Inflicted Maxillofacial Injuries: Treatment Modalities and Our Experience</a></span> The antibiotic of first choice for bite wounds is typically amoxicillin-clavulanic acid, which covers the common bacteria found in animal saliva.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="9"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-9" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-13" aria-label="Citation 9">9</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-13" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16055237/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Animal bite injuries to the head: 132 cases</a></span></p>
<p>If you are bitten, do not wait to see how it looks. Bite wounds to the face should be evaluated promptly. In addition to infection risk, the crushing and tearing nature of a bite can damage underlying muscle and nerves in ways that are not obvious at first glance. Rabies risk, while uncommon with domestic pets, also needs to be assessed based on the animal&#8217;s vaccination status.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio6 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="8124515562"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>The Hidden Problem of Tooth Fragments</h2>
<p>One complication of lip trauma that gets overlooked surprisingly often involves broken teeth. When a blow to the face fractures an upper front tooth, part of that tooth can be driven into the lower lip at the moment of impact and become embedded in the soft tissue. These fragments are not always easy to spot during a clinical exam, especially when there is swelling and bleeding.</p>
<p>If a tooth fragment is left inside the lip, it can cause chronic infection, a foreign-body reaction, discoloration of the overlying skin, and disfiguring scarring. The recommendation is to take radiographs of the soft tissue before closing any lip wound associated with dental trauma to rule out embedded fragments.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="11"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-11" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-14" aria-label="Citation 11">11</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-14" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5012040/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tooth fragment embedded in the lower lip for 10 months following dentoalveolar trauma: A case report with literature review</a></span> In some cases, a retained fragment is discovered months later when it triggers symptoms or shows up on an unrelated X-ray.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="12"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-12" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-15" aria-label="Citation 12">12</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-15" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15773891/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tooth fragment embedded in the lower lip after dental trauma: case reports</a></span> In pediatric cases, the fragment can sometimes be surgically retrieved and reattached to the broken tooth using adhesive techniques.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="13"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-13" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-16" aria-label="Citation 13">13</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-16" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3692182/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dental fragment embedded in the lower lip after facial trauma: Brief review literature and report of a case</a></span></p>
<p>The practical takeaway: if a lip injury happens alongside a chipped or broken tooth, mention the dental injury to the treating clinician even if the lip wound seems like the bigger problem. A quick X-ray could save a lot of trouble down the road.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio7 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="5498352226"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>Lip Injuries in Children</h2>
<p>Falls are the leading cause of lip lacerations in children, and the lower lip gets hit disproportionately often because of the way kids land face-first. Parents frequently face the question of whether their child&#8217;s lip wound needs stitches or can be managed at home, and clinicians face a parallel question about how to close the wound with minimal distress.</p>
<p>Tissue adhesive (medical-grade skin glue) has become an increasingly popular alternative to sutures for facial lacerations in children. A study comparing the two methods for facial wounds found that cosmetic outcomes were similar between tissue adhesive and sutures, but children treated with glue reported significantly less pain and their families reported greater satisfaction with the experience.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="14"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-14" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-17" aria-label="Citation 14">14</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-17" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10532459/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Comparative Evaluation of Tissue Adhesives and Sutures in the Management of Facial Laceration Wounds in Children</a></span> Complication rates for wound opening and infection were comparable between the two approaches.</p>
<p>That said, tissue adhesive has limitations. It does not work well for wounds under tension, full-thickness lip lacerations, or wounds that cross the vermilion border where precise alignment is critical. For those injuries, sutures remain the standard. The choice often comes down to wound characteristics rather than the child&#8217;s preference, though when glue is appropriate, it spares the child the experience of needles and stitches.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- Bio8 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="4797572628"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>Numbness and Nerve Recovery After Lip Trauma</h2>
<p>Significant lip injuries, especially those involving the lower lip, can damage branches of the trigeminal nerve that provide sensation to the lip and chin. The result is numbness, tingling, or altered sensation that can persist long after the wound itself has healed. This is understandably alarming, but the news is generally encouraging.</p>
<p>Research on sensory recovery after trigeminal nerve injury shows that sensation returns in a predictable sequence. Simple touch perception and the ability to feel the direction of a brushstroke recover first, often within weeks. Pain and temperature sensation follow. Fine spatial discrimination, the ability to tell apart closely spaced points on the skin, recovers last and can take many months.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="15"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-15" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-18" aria-label="Citation 15">15</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-18" role="note" hidden>Brain. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/117.1.149" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A psychophysical study of the mechanisms of sensory recovery following nerve injury in humans</a></span> Animal research suggests that serotonin activity in the nerve pathways plays a role in speeding this recovery process.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="16"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-16" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-19" aria-label="Citation 16">16</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-19" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12485414/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Serotonin promotes lip sensory recovery after inferior alveolar nerve transection via histone serotonylation</a></span></p>
<p>When nerve damage is severe enough to require microsurgical repair, about a third of patients regain full sensation, over half regain what is classified as useful sensory function, and roughly one in seven shows no meaningful recovery after a year.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="17"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-17" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-20" aria-label="Citation 17">17</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-20" role="note" hidden>Journal of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joms.2009.04.115" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsurgical Repair of Peripheral Trigeminal Nerve Injuries From Maxillofacial Trauma</a></span> For most lip lacerations that do not involve deep nerve transection, the prognosis for sensory recovery is considerably better than these surgical cases. If you notice persistent numbness weeks after a lip injury, bring it up with your clinician, as early assessment can guide whether watchful waiting or intervention is more appropriate.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- bio9 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="7303035204"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>Managing Scars After Lip Wounds</h2>
<p>Even with skilled repair, scars on and around the lip are a concern because the area is so visible. The lip&#8217;s healing advantages reduce but do not eliminate scarring on the outer skin surface, and any wound that crosses the vermilion border has the potential for a noticeable scar line.</p>
<p>Silicone-based scar treatments are the most widely studied non-surgical option. Silicone gel, applied topically once the wound has fully closed, helps flatten and soften developing scars. For lip scars specifically, silicone gel is generally preferred over silicone sheeting because sheeting on or near the mouth poses a choking hazard, especially in infants and young children, and both forms produce similar scar outcomes.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="18"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-18" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-21" aria-label="Citation 18">18</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-21" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5943303/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clinical evaluation of silicone gel in the treatment of cleft lip scars</a></span> For scars that become raised or hypertrophic, combining silicone gel with laser treatment has shown improvement beyond silicone gel alone, with lip scars responding particularly well to this combination.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="19"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-19" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-22" aria-label="Citation 19">19</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-22" role="note" hidden>PubMed. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39891719/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Silicone gel versus combination of silicone gel and a 577-nm diode laser in the treatment of post-surgery hypertrophic scar (comparative study)</a></span></p>
<p>Sun protection is another simple but effective measure. UV exposure darkens new scars, and lip skin is thin with relatively little melanin protection. Applying lip balm with SPF 30 or higher over healing wounds during the first year helps prevent permanent discoloration. If a scar remains cosmetically bothersome after it has fully matured, which takes roughly a year, revision procedures ranging from simple outpatient techniques like V-Y plasty or Z-plasty to more involved approaches are available.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="20"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-20" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-23" aria-label="Citation 20">20</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-23" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8705065/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Correction of whistle deformity secondary to cleft lip repair by V-Y plasty: a simple and effective technique</a></span></p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- bio10 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="8525064644"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>What Predicts a Poor Outcome</h2>
<p>A study examining risk factors for poor results in adults with full-thickness lip wounds found that older age, longer wounds (roughly four to eight centimeters), damage to tissues surrounding the wound, and scab formation over the repair site were all independently associated with worse outcomes.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="21"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-21" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-24" aria-label="Citation 21">21</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-24" role="note" hidden>Asian Journal of Surgery. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asjsur.2024.11.133" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Risk factors for poor outcomes in adult patients with lip through-and-through wounds</a></span> Interestingly, wound contamination and antibiotic use were not independent predictors of poor results once the other factors were accounted for, suggesting that wound characteristics and patient factors matter more than whether the wound started out dirty.</p>
<p>For aftercare, keeping the wound clean but moist appears more helpful than letting a thick scab form. Warm saltwater rinses for intraoral wounds, and gentle cleaning with petroleum jelly on the outer surface, are standard recommendations. Avoid stretching the lips wide open during the first week after repair, which means cutting food into small pieces and not taking big bites. Steer clear of anything very hot, spicy, or acidic, all of which irritate fresh wounds inside the mouth.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="4"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-4" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-25" aria-label="Citation 4">4</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-25" role="note" hidden>Emergency Medicine Clinics of North America. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0733-8627(05)70139-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lacerations of the Mouth &#8211; Section: Wound Dressing and Care</a></span></p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- bio11 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="1931289022"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>When Major Lip Tissue Is Lost</h2>
<p>Most lip lacerations involve cuts that can be stitched back together. But severe trauma, aggressive tumor removal, or devastating bite injuries can result in loss of a substantial portion of the lip itself. These cases require reconstructive surgery rather than simple wound closure.</p>
<p>For defects involving up to about a third of the lip, the remaining tissue can usually be advanced or rearranged to close the gap. Larger defects, those involving two thirds or more, call for more complex techniques. The Abbe flap is one of the most established methods: a segment of the opposite lip (upper lip tissue to fill a lower lip defect, or vice versa) is rotated on its blood supply to fill the gap.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="22"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-22" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-26" aria-label="Citation 22">22</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-26" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5429119/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lower lip reconstruction using a skin-mucosa Abbe-Estlander flap after squamous cell carcinoma excision</a></span> For very large defects, surgeons sometimes combine multiple flap techniques to reconstruct the lip while preserving the ability to open and close the mouth.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="23"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-23" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-27" aria-label="Citation 23">23</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-27" role="note" hidden>Archives of Craniofacial Surgery. <a href="https://doi.org/10.7181/acfs.2021.00493" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reconstruction of a large lower lip defect using a combination of Abbe and staircase flaps: a case report</a></span> The lip&#8217;s generous blood supply makes these transferred tissue flaps less prone to failure from poor circulation than similar flaps used elsewhere on the body.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- bio12 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="9618207354"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>The Emotional Weight of Lip and Facial Injuries</h2>
<p>It is easy to focus entirely on the physical healing and overlook the psychological impact. The lips are central to facial expression, communication, eating, and intimacy, and visible scarring in this area can affect how people see themselves and interact with others.</p>
<p>Research comparing facial trauma patients with the general population has found significantly lower life satisfaction, a more negative perception of body image, and higher rates of depression and post-traumatic stress among those with facial scarring.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="24"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-24" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-28" aria-label="Citation 24">24</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-28" role="note" hidden>Annals of Plastic Surgery. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1097/01.sap.0000155282.48465.94" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quality of Life and Facial Trauma</a></span> These effects extend beyond mood, with associations found between facial trauma and higher rates of unemployment and relationship problems.</p>
<p>If you or someone you care for has sustained a significant lip injury, it is worth knowing that psychological support is a legitimate part of recovery, not a sign of overreacting. Many plastic surgery and maxillofacial clinics now incorporate counseling or referrals into the follow-up process, recognizing that restoring how a person feels about their face matters alongside the tissue repair itself.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- bio13 -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="2290381673"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><h2>Lip Injuries as a Warning Sign in Children</h2>
<p>Pediatric lip injuries usually result from the ordinary tumbles of childhood, but clinicians are trained to consider the possibility of non-accidental injury when the history does not match the wound pattern. Oral and facial trauma is present in roughly half of physically abused children, and the lips, tongue, and oral mucosa are frequently involved. Torn lip frenula (the small tissue bands connecting the lip to the gum) in infants and injuries that look inconsistent with the reported mechanism deserve careful evaluation.<sup class="curio-fn" data-fn="25"><a class="curio-fn__ref" href="#curio-cite-25" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="curio-fn-29" aria-label="Citation 25">25</a></sup><span class="curio-fn__note" id="curio-fn-29" role="note" hidden>PubMed Central. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4969728/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oral and dental signs of child abuse and neglect</a></span> This is not to alarm parents whose child falls and busts a lip, which happens constantly. Rather, it is context for why clinicians may ask detailed questions about how a child&#8217;s lip injury occurred.</p>
<script async src="https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<!-- BioBottom -->
<ins class="adsbygoogle"
     style="display:block"
     data-ad-client="ca-pub-7959640701186883"
     data-ad-slot="1805761050"
     data-ad-format="auto"
     data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
<script>
     (adsbygoogle = window.adsbygoogle || []).push({});
</script><!-- CONTENT END 1 -->
</div>
